# DevOps Labs

Hands-on DevOps projects: Linux, Git, build tools, cloud infrastructure, artifact repositories, containers and CI/CD. Each project is documented with its own README.

> **Status: work in progress.** I am working through a self-paced DevOps bootcamp and committing each module as I complete it. This root README is a placeholder and will be rewritten when the programme is finished.

## About this repository

This is a single monorepo holding every practical project I build while training as a DevOps engineer. The projects are hands-on follow-along bootcamp exercises: I completed each step myself on my own environment, and I documented each one as I went.

The documentation is as much a part of the work as the code. Every project folder contains a README explaining what the project does, why the important steps were taken, how the result was verified, and what I took away from it. Some projects are documentation-only — where the application code was the instructor's demo, committing it would add nothing, so the README stands on its own.

## Repository structure

```
.
├── README.md
└── module-NN-short-name/
    ├── README.md            # module index: scope, shared context, project list
    └── NN-project-name/
        ├── README.md        # the project write-up
        ├── app/             # application source, when committed
        ├── screenshots/     # only when screenshots add real evidence
        └── docs/            # only when supporting documents are needed
```

Each module folder has its own README acting as an index. Start there, then open the individual project READMEs.

## Modules

| Module | Topic                                  | Status      |
| ------ | -------------------------------------- | ----------- |
| 01     | Introduction to DevOps                 | Complete    |
| 02     | Operating systems & Linux basics       | Complete    |
| 03     | Version control with Git               | Complete    |
| 04     | Build tools & package managers         | Complete    |
| 05     | Cloud & IaaS basics                    | Complete    |
| 06     | Artifact repository manager with Nexus | Complete    |
| 07     | Containers with Docker                 | Complete    |
| 08     | Build automation & CI/CD with Jenkins  | In progress |

Later modules — Kubernetes, AWS services, Terraform, monitoring — will be added as I reach them.

## Conventions

- One folder per module, lowercase kebab-case: `module-NN-short-name`
- One subfolder per project, numbered where the projects form an ordered sequence
- Three README levels: repository, module, project
- No credentials, keys, tokens or sensitive addresses anywhere in the repository; sensitive values appear as descriptive placeholders such as `<server-ip>` or `<username>`

## A note on accuracy

These are learning projects, not original designs. Where a project follows an instructor's demo application, the README says so. I have deliberately avoided inflating any of the write-ups: if a detail was not recorded, it is described at a lower level of precision or left out rather than invented.
